Write a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to build a
Python script which, given a path to an MKV video file and an optional output
directory, locates and downloads the best English subtitles for that video and
saves them alongside or in the specified directory.
Specify the following: Purpose: A command-line utility for Termux (Android) that
fetches English subtitles for a local MKV video using the subliminal library and
babelfish for language codes.
Environment and shebang: Use the Termux Python path shebang
(`#!/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python`) and include `from
__future__ import annotations` at the top.
Dependencies: `babelfish`, `subliminal` (including
`subliminal.providers.ProviderError` and `subliminal.video.scan_video`, plus
`download_best_subtitles` and `save_subtitles`), plus standard library
`logging`, `sys`, and `pathlib.Path`.
Logging: Configure root logging at INFO level with the format `%(asctime)s -
%(levelname)s - %(message)s`, and create a module-level logger using `__name__`.
Core function `get_english_subtitles(mkv_path, output_dir=None)`: - Convert the
input to a `Path`.
If the file does not exist, log an error and return `False`. - If the suffix is
not `.mkv` (case-insensitive), log a warning but continue processing. - If
`output_dir` is not given, default to the video's parent directory.
Otherwise convert it to a `Path` and create it (including parents) if missing. -
Print the scanning message, then call `scan_video`.
On success print the video name, size in bytes, duration formatted to two
decimal places in seconds, and hashes.
On any exception, log an error and return `False`. - Create the language object
with `babelfish.Language("eng")` and print that English subtitles are being
searched. - Call `download_best_subtitles` for the scanned video with English as
the only requested language, handling `ProviderError` and other exceptions with
logged errors and a `False` return. - If no subtitles are found, log a message
and return `False`.
Otherwise save the best subtitle(s) with `save_subtitles` into the output
directory, log each saved file, and return `True` on success.
Entry point: When run as a script, read the MKV path from the first command-line
argument (via `sys.argv`) and optionally the output directory from the second
argument.
If no path is given, print a usage message and exit with a non-zero status.
Call `get_english_subtitles` and exit with status 0 on success or 1 on failure.
Edge cases to handle: nonexistent files, non-MKV extensions (warning only),
missing or newly created output directories, scan failures, provider errors,
network failures during subtitle search, and no subtitles found.
Use `Path` consistently and avoid hard-coded absolute paths except the shebang.
---
